\widctlpar\intbl\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls14\adjustright\rin0\lin720\pararsid11022883\yts15\contextualspace \rtlch\fcs1 \af31507\afs22\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \f31506\fs22\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0\afs24 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\fs24\insrsid8849958\charrsid11022883 Nature of the Disease: }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\fs24\insrsid9592980\charrsid11022883 
Lumpy Skin Disease (LSD) is a highly contagious, vector-borne, and economically significant transboundary disease of cattle, caused by the Lumpy Skin Disease Virus (LSDV) of the Poxviridae family. Outbreaks are often seasonal, peaking during and after the
 rainy season when populations of insect vectors (mosquitoes, biting flies) are highest. Clinical signs include the development of characteristic firm, raised skin nodules on the head, neck, limbs, udder, and perineum. These nodules can ulcerate, leading 
to
 severe secondary bacterial infections. Systemic illness causes high fever, lymphadenopathy, pronounced milk drop (agalactia) in lactating cows, temporary infertility in bulls, and respiratory distress, resulting in significant morbidity, mortality, and e
conomic loss.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \s16 \intbl\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f3\insrsid8849958\charrsid11022883 \loch\af3\dbch\af31506\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\fs24\insrsid8849958\charrsid11022883 Transmission:}{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0\afs24 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\fs24\insrsid8849958\charrsid11022883  }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\fs24\insrsid9592980\charrsid11022883 LSD is primarily transmitted mechanically by blood-feeding arthropod vectors, including mosquitoes (Culicoides
 spp.), biting flies (e.g., stable flies), and ticks. It can also spread through direct contact with lesions, secretions, or contaminated fomites (feed, water, equipment) and iatrogenically via contaminated needles or surgical instruments.}{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0\afs24 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\fs24\insrsid8849958\charrsid11022883 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \s16 \intbl\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f3\insrsid9592980\charrsid11022883 \loch\af3\dbch\af31506\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\fs24\insrsid9592980\charrsid11022883 Clinical Signs:}{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0\afs24 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\fs24\insrsid9592980\charrsid11022883 
 The pathognomonic sign is the eruption of multiple cutaneous nodules over the entire body. These nodules involve the full thickness of the skin (dermis) and can develop into deep, necrotic ulcers. Other systemic signs include pyrexia (high fever), depres
sion, anorexia, weight loss, lameness, lymphadenitis, and mucopurulent ocular and nasal discharge. Survivors may have permanent scarring or hide damage as nodules slough. Any suspected case must be reported immediately to veterinary authorities.}{
